## 3. Core Java Topics — Deep but Practical

---

### Variables and Data Types

```java
// Primitive types — fast, stored on stack
int age = 25;
long population = 8_000_000_000L;
double salary = 75000.50;
boolean isActive = true;
char grade = 'A';

// Reference types — stored on heap
String name = "Alice";
int[] scores = {90, 85, 78};
```

**Backend relevance:** JSON fields map to Java types. An `id` field is `Long`. A `price` is `BigDecimal` (not `double` — precision matters for money). A status is `boolean` or `enum`.

> ⚠️ Use `BigDecimal` for financial calculations. Never `double`.

---

### Loops and Conditions

Know all three loop types and when to use each:

```java
// For loop — when you know the count
for (int i = 0; i < users.size(); i++) { ... }

// Enhanced for — cleaner iteration
for (User user : users) { ... }

// Stream (modern Java) — functional style
users.stream().filter(u -> u.isActive()).collect(Collectors.toList());
```

**Backend relevance:** Filtering a list of records from a database, iterating over request items in a batch API.

---

### Methods

```java
// Method signature anatomy
public ResponseEntity<User> getUserById(Long id) throws ResourceNotFoundException {
    // ...
}
```

Key concepts:
- **Method overloading** — same name, different parameters (used in service layers)
- **Return types** — always explicit in Java
- **`void`** — when a method only performs an action (e.g., `sendEmail()`)

---

### OOP — The Four Pillars

#### Encapsulation

```java
public class User {
    private String email;      // hidden
    private String password;   // hidden

    public String getEmail() { return email; }
    public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }
    // No getter for password — intentional
}
```

**Backend relevance:** You never expose internal state directly. Passwords are never returned in API responses.

#### Inheritance

```java
public class BaseEntity {
    private Long id;
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;
}

public class User extends BaseEntity {
    private String username;
    private String email;
}

public class Product extends BaseEntity {
    private String name;
    private BigDecimal price;
}
```

**Backend relevance:** A common `BaseEntity` avoids repeating `id`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` in every database model.

#### Polymorphism

```java
public interface NotificationService {
    void send(String message, String recipient);
}

public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    public void send(String message, String recipient) { /* send email */ }
}

public class SMSNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    public void send(String message, String recipient) { /* send SMS */ }
}
```

**Backend relevance:** Your code depends on the interface, not the implementation. Swap `EmailService` for `SMSService` without changing business logic.

#### Abstraction

```java
public abstract class PaymentProcessor {
    public final void processPayment(double amount) {
        validate(amount);
        execute(amount);
        notify();
    }
    
    protected abstract void execute(double amount); // subclass decides HOW
    
    private void validate(double amount) { /* common validation */ }
    private void notify() { /* common notification */ }
}
```

**Backend relevance:** Template method pattern — define the skeleton of an algorithm, let subclasses fill in details.

---

### Exception Handling

```java
// Custom exception — always create these for your domain
public class ResourceNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public ResourceNotFoundException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

// Service layer
public User findById(Long id) {
    return userRepository.findById(id)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("User not found with id: " + id));
}

// Global handler
@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(404).body(new ErrorResponse(ex.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

**Rule:** Never catch and ignore exceptions. Never expose raw stack traces to API consumers.

