### Java Memory Model (Conceptual Only)

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    JVM Memory                    │
│                                                  │
│  ┌────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────┐  │
│  │   STACK    │        │        HEAP          │  │
│  │            │        │                      │  │
│  │ primitives │        │  objects (new ...)   │  │
│  │ references │───────▶│  arrays              │  │
│  │ method     │        │  String pool         │  │
│  │ frames     │        │                      │  │
│  └────────────┘        └──────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**What you need to know:**
- Primitives live on the stack; objects live on the heap
- Garbage Collector automatically frees unreferenced heap objects
- Memory leaks happen when you hold references to objects you no longer need (e.g., filling a static `List` endlessly)
- `OutOfMemoryError` = heap is full

> You don't need to manually manage memory in Java. But knowing this helps you debug production issues.

