## 4. Data Structures & Algorithms in Java

---

### Why DSA Matters for Backend Engineers

| Backend Scenario | DSA Concept Used |
|-----------------|-----------------|
| Caching user sessions | `HashMap` — O(1) lookup |
| Rate limiting by user | `LinkedHashMap` with LRU policy |
| Processing a job queue | `Queue` / `Deque` |
| Finding shortest path (routing) | Graph + BFS |
| Autocomplete feature | Trie |
| Maintaining a sorted leaderboard | `TreeMap` / `PriorityQueue` |
| Detecting duplicate requests | `HashSet` |

You will face DSA in every technical interview. Strong DSA skills also make you write more efficient backend code.

---

### Core Data Structures

#### Arrays

```java
int[] arr = {5, 3, 8, 1, 9};
Arrays.sort(arr);                         // O(n log n)
int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 8);   // O(log n) — only on sorted arrays

// Two-pointer technique (used in many interview problems)
int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
    // process...
    left++; right--;
}
```

#### LinkedList

```java
LinkedList<Integer> list = new LinkedList<>();
list.addFirst(1);   // O(1)
list.addLast(2);    // O(1)
list.removeFirst(); // O(1)
// Random access: O(n) — don't use like ArrayList
```

#### Stack and Queue

```java
// Stack — LIFO (undo operations, expression evaluation)
Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
stack.push(1);
stack.pop();
stack.peek();

// Queue — FIFO (job queues, BFS)
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer("task1");
queue.poll();
queue.peek();
```

#### HashMap

```java
Map<String, Integer> wordCount = new HashMap<>();
wordCount.put("hello", 1);
wordCount.getOrDefault("world", 0);  // safe get
wordCount.putIfAbsent("hello", 2);   // only puts if not present

// Iterate entries
for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : wordCount.entrySet()) {
    System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + entry.getValue());
}
```

#### Trees and Graphs (Conceptual)

- **Binary Tree** — hierarchical structure, each node has max 2 children
- **Binary Search Tree (BST)** — left < root < right, O(log n) search
- **Graph** — nodes + edges; used in social networks, routing, dependency resolution
- **BFS** — level-by-level traversal; finding shortest path
- **DFS** — depth-first; used in recursion, backtracking

---

### Time and Space Complexity

| Complexity | Name | Example |
|-----------|------|---------|
| O(1) | Constant | `HashMap.get()`, array index access |
| O(log n) | Logarithmic | Binary search, BST lookup |
| O(n) | Linear | Single loop, `ArrayList.contains()` |
| O(n log n) | Linearithmic | `Arrays.sort()`, merge sort |
| O(n²) | Quadratic | Nested loops (avoid in production) |
| O(2ⁿ) | Exponential | Brute-force recursion (never in production) |

**Rule:** In backend, any operation that is O(n²) or worse on large datasets will cause performance problems. Know the complexity of what you're writing.

