## 9. What NOT to Learn

---

### Outdated Java Topics — Skip These

| Topic | Why Skip |
|-------|---------|
| **Java AWT / Swing** | Desktop GUI — irrelevant for backend |
| **Applets** | Deprecated and removed from Java |
| **`Date` and `Calendar` classes** | Replaced by `java.time` (Java 8+). Use `LocalDate`, `LocalDateTime` |
| **`StringBuffer`** | Use `StringBuilder` instead (faster for single-threaded code) |
| **`Vector` and `Hashtable`** | Legacy, replaced by `ArrayList` and `HashMap` |
| **RMI (Remote Method Invocation)** | Old distributed systems tech — not used in modern backends |
| **EJBs (Enterprise JavaBeans)** | Old enterprise Java — Spring replaced this entirely |
| **JNDI** | Only relevant in legacy enterprise systems |
| **Raw JDBC with manual SQL** | Learn it conceptually; use JPA/Spring Data in practice |
| **XML-based Spring configuration** | Modern Spring uses annotations. Skip XML configs entirely |

---

### Things to Avoid Early

| Mistake | Why It Hurts |
|---------|-------------|
| **Learning multiple frameworks before Core Java** | Spring Boot is hard to understand without solid OOP and Core Java |
| **Jumping to microservices** | Build a solid monolith first. Microservices add complexity without adding learning |
| **Over-engineering projects** | You don't need Kafka, Kubernetes, or GraphQL in your first project |
| **Memorizing syntax** | Java syntax is Google-able. Understand concepts, not syntax by heart |
| **Skipping OOP** | Developers who skip OOP write procedural Java — immediately obvious in interviews |
| **Using Lombok before understanding what it replaces** | Use Lombok after you've written 20+ classes manually |
| **Starting with reactive programming (WebFlux)** | Learn blocking Spring MVC first. Reactive is an advanced optimization |
| **Building frontend before backend is solid** | Stay backend-focused until you're confident |

---

### Framework Order (Do NOT Skip Steps)

```
WRONG order:  Spring Boot → Hibernate → Microservices → Kafka
              (you don't understand what any of it does)

RIGHT order:  Java Core → OOP → Collections → Exception Handling
              → Spring Boot MVC → JPA → Spring Security
              → Testing → Docker → Advanced topics
```

